---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// V-701 — incident response + customer-facing policy docs.
//
// Pinned by tests/unit/incident-policy-doc-parity.test.ts. Major fixes
// vs. prior revision: removed fictional `incident_subscriptions` account
// field, removed claim that `incident.*` are subscribable webhook
// events (they aren't — they're admin-audit / SSE-broadcast events),
// fixed /v1/status/sla response shape (camelCase, `data:` envelope),
// removed fictional `?window_days` query param.
---

<BaseLayout title="Incidents & SLA — Driftstack" description="What happens during a Driftstack incident: status page, severity ladder, customer comms, and SLA credit policy.">
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12 prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert">
    <h1>Incidents &amp; SLA</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      This page covers what happens during a Driftstack outage: how
      we detect, communicate, and resolve incidents, and the credit
      policy if we miss our SLA.
    </p>

    <h2>Status page</h2>
    <p>
      <strong><a href="https://status.driftstack.dev">status.driftstack.dev</a></strong>
      is the single source of truth during an incident. The same
      data the status page renders is also published via
      <code>GET /v1/status</code> (overall + per-component status)
      and <code>GET /v1/status/incidents</code> (recent / live
      incidents). To get notified by email when an incident is
      filed or resolved, subscribe via
      <code>POST /v1/status/subscribe</code> — see
      <a href="/docs/status-subscriptions/">/docs/status-subscriptions</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Severity ladder</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Severity</th><th>Definition</th><th>First update</th><th>Update cadence</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Outage</strong></td>
          <td>Core API down across all customers, or data-loss risk.</td>
          <td>≤ 15 min</td>
          <td>Every 30 min until resolved.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Major</strong></td>
          <td>API degraded ({'>'}5% error rate) OR a critical surface
            (auth, sessions) unavailable for a subset of customers.</td>
          <td>≤ 30 min</td>
          <td>Every 60 min.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Minor</strong></td>
          <td>Single non-critical surface (dashboard, an SDK build pipeline) degraded.</td>
          <td>≤ 60 min</td>
          <td>At resolution.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Maintenance</strong> (not an incident severity)</td>
          <td>Planned change with potential impact. Always announced
            ≥48h in advance. Announced by email and a status post; it is
            not one of the three incident severities above.</td>
          <td>Pre-announced</td>
          <td>Start + end.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Detection</h2>
    <p>
      Three signals trigger an incident:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Automated health probes:</strong> 60-second poller
        against <code>/health</code> + per-region API endpoints.
        Three consecutive failures auto-create a <strong>Major</strong>
        incident. Escalation to <strong>Outage</strong> is a human
        judgement — automation never files one, so a probe trip alone
        will not turn the status page red.</li>
      <li><strong>Customer reports:</strong> emails to
        <a href="mailto:support@driftstack.dev">support@driftstack.dev</a>
        and Slack channel monitoring. We acknowledge within 30 min
        during EU business hours.</li>
      <li><strong>Internal alerting:</strong> Sentry + cost-monitoring
        thresholds page on-call. Anything that warrants customer
        comms gets escalated to a public status-page entry.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Customer communications during an incident</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Status page entry filed</strong> with severity +
        title + affected components.</li>
      <li><strong>Email fan-out</strong> to confirmed
        <code>/v1/status/subscribe</code> subscribers.</li>
      <li><strong>Progress updates</strong> on the cadence above.
        Each update appends to the incident's timeline (visible via
        <code>GET /v1/status/incidents</code>).</li>
      <li><strong>Resolution</strong> marks the incident resolved and
        triggers a final email to subscribers with a root-cause
        summary + remediation steps.</li>
      <li><strong>Postmortem</strong> for Outage / Major incidents
        published within 7 business days on the
        <a href="https://status.driftstack.dev">public status page</a>
        as a permanent entry under the resolved incident. Minor
        incidents get an inline summary on the resolved status entry.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <strong>Note:</strong> <code>incident.created</code> /
      <code>incident.updated</code> / <code>incident.resolved</code>
      are admin-audit / internal SSE event types, not customer webhook
      subscription values. Email subscription is the customer-facing
      notification path.
    </p>

    <h2>SLA + credit policy</h2>
    <p>
      Driftstack's SLA is on the <strong>API control plane</strong>
      (session create + lifecycle endpoints). The dashboard and SDK
      distribution are best-effort.
    </p>
    <p>
      Tier-by-tier SLA targets, the windowing methodology, the
      credit bands, and the dispute process all live in
      <a href="/docs/sla-policy/">/docs/sla-policy</a> — that is the
      authoritative reference. Tier identifiers used there match
      the <code>AccountTier</code> enum exactly.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading SLA data from the API</h2>
    <pre><code>{`GET /v1/status/sla

→ {
  "data": [
    {
      "target": "api.driftstack.dev",
      "uptimePct": 99.99,
      "totalProbes": 43200,
      "okCount": 43196,
      "failCount": 4,
      "lastProbeAt": "2026-05-11T13:00:00Z",
      "lastFailureAt": "2026-05-09T14:23:00Z",
      "windowStart": "2026-04-11T13:00:00Z",
      "windowEnd": "2026-05-11T13:00:00Z"
    }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      No auth — status surface is public. Window is a fixed rolling
      30 days. Field names are camelCase (the SLA report serialises
      its internal model directly).
    </p>

    <h2>Postmortems</h2>
    <p>
      Public postmortems for Outage + Major incidents live on the
      <a href="https://status.driftstack.dev">public status page</a>,
      attached to the resolved incident entry. Each follows the same
      template: timeline, root cause, what we changed to prevent
      recurrence. Postmortems are blameless and detailed enough to be
      useful — we'd rather over-share than under-share.
    </p>

    <h2>Reporting a problem</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Acute outage:</strong> if the status page doesn't
        already show the incident, email
        <a href="mailto:urgent@driftstack.dev">urgent@driftstack.dev</a>.
        That goes straight to on-call.</li>
      <li><strong>Non-acute bug / weird behaviour:</strong>
        <a href="mailto:support@driftstack.dev">support@driftstack.dev</a>
        with a session id + timestamp.</li>
      <li><strong>Security:</strong>
        <a href="mailto:security@driftstack.dev">security@driftstack.dev</a>
        — PGP available on the page. See also
        <a href="/docs/api-security-headers/">/docs/api-security-headers</a>
        for the response-header reference reviewers ask about.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Related</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/docs/sla-policy/">SLA policy (authoritative tier targets + credit bands)</a></li>
      <li><a href="/docs/status-subscriptions/">Status email subscriptions</a></li>
      <li><a href="/docs/webhooks/">Webhook signing + retries (for the events that are subscribable today)</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://status.driftstack.dev">Status page</a></li>
    </ul>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
